    name: 'Offer',
    short: 'A definite promise capable of acceptance, the first ingredient of a common-law contract.',
    extended: '<p>An <strong>offer</strong> is a definite promise capable of acceptance &mdash; specific enough that, when assented to without modification, it forms a binding contract. The classic test is whether a reasonable person would think the offeror intended to be bound on those terms.</p><p>An offer must be distinguished from an <em>invitation to treat</em> (e.g. goods on a shop shelf, an advertisement) which merely invites others to make offers. In M&amp;A, an indicative offer letter is typically not a legal "offer" in the contract-formation sense because it is expressly non-binding.</p>',
