    extended: '<p><strong>Consideration</strong> is the doctrine that a common-law contract requires something of value moving from each side. Per <em>Currie v Misa</em> (1875) LR 10 Ex 153: <em>"some right, interest, profit or benefit accruing to the one party, or some forbearance, detriment, loss or responsibility given, suffered or undertaken by the other."</em></p><p>Consideration need not be adequate (a peppercorn suffices) but must be "sufficient" (real and not illusory). Civil-law systems (France, Germany, etc.) do not use consideration; an offer + acceptance forms a contract without it.</p><p>To make a one-sided promise binding in English law without consideration, the parties execute a <strong>deed</strong>.</p>',
