    short: 'Civil-law concept of the legal-economic reason for an obligation; abolished in France in 2016 but retained in some other civilian systems.',
    extended: '<p><strong>Cause</strong> (French: <em>cause</em>; Italian: <em>causa</em>; Latin origin: <em>causa</em>) was the civil-law analogue of consideration: the legal-economic reason why a party undertakes an obligation. France abolished <em>cause</em> as a formal contract requirement in the 2016 reform of contract law (Ordonnance no. 2016-131 of 10 February 2016), replacing it with a flexible test of "content" (<em>contenu</em>) and a lawful, certain object.</p><p>Germany never had <em>cause</em> in the French sense; under the BGB, a contract is formed by <em>Angebot</em> (offer) and <em>Annahme</em> (acceptance), full stop.</p>',
