    short: 'Delaware case — cyclical industry downturn not durationally significant; specific performance ordered.',
    extended: '<p><em>In re IBP, Inc. Shareholders Litigation</em>, 789 A.2d 14 (Del. Ch. 2001). Vice Chancellor Strine refused Tyson\'s attempt to walk from the IBP acquisition citing MAE. IBP\'s Q1 earnings had fallen 64% but in a cyclical industry known to be cyclical at Signing. The court coined the canonical formulation: the MAE clause is "best read as a backstop protecting the acquiror from the occurrence of unknown events that substantially threaten the overall earnings potential of the target in a durationally-significant manner." Specific performance ordered.</p>',
    juris: ['us'],
