    short: 'Cross-module map showing how each structural choice (share/asset/scheme/offer/§251(h)/RTM/FTM) cascades into specific SPA mechanics, regulatory-CP placement, price-mechanic viability, indemnity scope and W&I pricing.',
    extended: '<p>The structural choice in Module 06 dictates a chain of downstream consequences in Modules 07&ndash;14. The matrix below maps the principal cascades:</p><ul><li><strong>Share deal</strong> &rarr; broader R&amp;W package (Module 12) because the buyer inherits all unknown liabilities; W&amp;I premium higher because the policy underwrites the full warranty set (Module 14); locked-box and completion accounts both available (Module 11); CoC consents on critical contracts populate the third-party-consent CP list (Module 08).</li><li><strong>Asset deal</strong> &rarr; narrower R&amp;Ws but heavier <em>specific indemnities</em> for excluded liabilities (Module 13); tax DD focuses on apportionment to assets (step-up) rather than entity history; almost always run on completion accounts (Module 11) because the asset perimeter and working-capital allocation must be finalised at completion; W&amp;I priced differently because the underlying liability transfer mechanic is asset-by-asset, not entity-wide.</li><li><strong>Scheme of arrangement</strong> &rarr; cash-confirmation under Code Rule 24.8 feeds into the SPA&rsquo;s certain-funds covenant (Module 08); financing is on LMA "certain funds" terms; PSL and class composition drive the Court Meeting timetable; no squeeze-out needed (100% in one step).</li><li><strong>Contractual offer</strong> &rarr; squeeze-out at 90% under CA 2006 &sect;979 (otherwise minority remains); cash-only if Rule 11 triggered; faster than scheme but riskier; allows interloper bid mid-offer.</li><li><strong>One-step DGCL &sect;251 merger</strong> &rarr; proxy-driven 12&ndash;16-week timetable; &sect;251(c) majority-of-outstanding vote; appraisal under &sect;262 looms.</li><li><strong>Two-step &sect;251(h)</strong> &rarr; 5&ndash;6-week timetable; <em>Corwin</em> cleansing through tender acceptance; eligibility conditions tight (listed or 2,000+ stockholders, any-and-all, same consideration, explicit contemplation).</li><li><strong>Forward triangular merger (FTM)</strong> &rarr; target dissolves into Sub; "by operation of law" anti-assignment language often triggered (<em>MTA Canada</em>); contract review in DD (Module 04) decides whether FTM is viable.</li><li><strong>Reverse triangular merger (RTM)</strong> &rarr; target survives; contracts continue (<em>Meso Scale</em>); dominant US public-company structure; combined with IRC &sect;368(a)(2)(E) tax-free reorganisation.</li></ul><p>For partner-fluency: a senior M&amp;A lawyer runs this matrix as a 90-second decision tree at the start of every structuring call. The user&rsquo;s job is to recognise which branch the conversation has just moved to.</p>',
    juris: ['us', 'uk', 'eu'],
